# ~/.claude — public config repo (a.k.a. "dotclaude")
#
# Strategy: IGNORE EVERYTHING by default, then allow-list only the
# portable, secret-free config. ~/.claude's working tree also holds
# credentials and session state, so a deny-all default is what makes a
# PUBLIC repo here safe: `git add -A` can never stage anything that
# isn't explicitly un-ignored below.

# 1) Ignore every top-level entry…
/*

# 2) …then allow only these portable, secret-free paths:
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes
!/README.md
# Claude Code's own settings: effort level, TUI, theme, prompt toggles. Small,
# credential-free, and exactly what you want back after a migration.
# settings.local.json stays out: machine-local by convention, and it carries
# absolute paths from whichever host wrote it.
!/settings.json
!/skills/
!/agents/
!/commands/
!/hooks/

# Reminder: skills/agents/commands/hooks are allow-listed WHOLESALE —
# never drop a secret inside them or it WILL be tracked.

# NEVER allow-list these (here as a reminder, already ignored by /*):
#   .credentials.json   history.jsonl   sessions/   projects/   todos/
#   telemetry/  statsig/  cache/  shell-snapshots/
#   CLAUDE.md   (deliberately machine-/personal-specific)
# settings.json was on this list until 2026-07-30. Reversed deliberately: its
# contents are effortLevel, tui, theme and a prompt toggle, which are personal
# preference rather than machine state, and are exactly what you want back after
# a migration. settings.local.json keeps the original reasoning and stays out.

# Never track Python bytecode or OS cruft, even inside allow-listed dirs
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.DS_Store
